= Learning =

== Overview ==
'''Learning''' is a relatively durable change in knowledge, skill, representation, expectation, or behavior resulting from experience, practice, instruction, or adaptation.

Learning is inferred from changes over time; a single moment of improved performance does not by itself establish learning.

== Forms of learning ==
Research distinguishes many forms, including:

* associative learning;
* classical conditioning;
* operant/instrumental learning;
* perceptual learning;
* motor learning;
* category learning;
* statistical learning;
* observational/social learning;
* concept and rule learning.

These categories overlap and use different experimental methods.

== Learning and memory ==
[[Memory]] allows past experience to influence later cognition and behavior.

Learning is the process or change produced by experience; memory is one set of mechanisms through which that change can be retained or expressed.

The concepts are closely connected but not identical.

== Practice and feedback ==
Practice can improve performance, especially when it supports retrieval, error correction, varied application, and appropriate feedback.

Repeated exposure alone is not guaranteed to produce robust transferable learning.

== Generalization ==
Learning can remain tied to the original context or generalize to new examples and situations.

Transfer depends on similarity, abstraction, prior knowledge, strategy, and whether learners recognize relevant structure.

== Learning and machine learning ==
Human and animal learning are biological/cognitive phenomena.

[[Machine learning]] uses algorithms that change model behavior from data or experience.

The analogy is useful but does not make the mechanisms identical.

